1. What a cloud session is
A cloud session is a Claude Code session that runs on Anthropic’s servers, away from your laptop. Each one gets a fresh virtual machine (a computer simulated inside a bigger one, thrown away afterwards) with a fresh clone (a full copy of a repository) of your code, made from its copy on GitHub. It keeps working after you close your laptop. When it is done, the work comes back as a branch, which you can turn into a pull request (a proposal on GitHub to merge that branch, with a place to review the changes).
Only committed files (changes you have saved into git history with git commit) travel, because the clone is made from GitHub. A committed CLAUDE.md (the instructions file Claude Code reads at the start of a session) and the project’s skills and agents come along. What stays behind:

your personal ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (the same kind of file, kept in your home folder), skills, agents and user-level settings;
MCP servers (add-on connections that give the agent extra tools) you added only for yourself;
.claude/settings.local.json and interactive sign-ins.

The permission rules
The agent’s harness (the program around the model) enforces permission rules, checking deny first, then ask, then allow.

Claude CodeOpenCode + Ollama


A teaching configuration. Put it in .claude/settings.json in your project folder (shared with the team) or .claude/settings.local.json (only you), or in ~/.claude/settings.json for all projects:
{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": ["Bash(squeue *)", "Bash(sinfo *)", "Bash(sacct *)"],
    "ask":   ["Bash(sbatch *)", "Bash(srun *)", "Bash(salloc *)", "Bash(scancel *)"],
    "deny":  ["Bash(scancel -u *)", "Bash(scancel --user*)", "Bash(scancel --me*)"]
  }
}
An ask rule prompts even when a narrower allow rule matches. Ask and deny rules apply to every part of a compound command (&&, ;, |, loops, $(...)).
Bash rules are not a security boundary. Bash(rm *) does not stop /bin/rm or bash -c 'rm ...', and a scancel rule can be bypassed the same way. For real enforcement use a PreToolUse hook (a script the harness runs before each tool call; the sandbox limits files and network and leaves Slurm commands alone). Treat the rules above as a prompt that keeps you in the loop.


OpenCode accepts allow, ask and deny, and an unmatched action asks. Its base policy begins with an allow-all rule, so sbatch runs without a prompt until you add a rule. opencode.jsonc is OpenCode’s configuration file. Create or open it in your project folder, check your installed version’s permission docs, then add this to its permissions array:
"permissions": [
  { "action": "shell", "resource": "sbatch *", "effect": "ask" }
]
Add similar rules for srun, salloc and scancel, and a deny for scancel -u * and scancel --me*. Rules are ordered, so check where they sit relative to the broad allow. As in Claude Code, a command that no rule matches goes through, and shell rules do not sandbox anything.
